This HAT replaces the loose Dupont wires of the [current setup](../wiring-pi3/#2-light--esp32-and-led-strip).
It is a small perfboard (**65 × 51 mm, 25 × 20 holes**) that plugs on the Amp2's 40 pins **by a narrow strip along the header only**.
The Amp2 has components too tall for a full-size HAT, so the rest of the board **hangs beyond the edge of the Pi, in the air**, and carries:

- the **30-pin ESP32 devkit** that runs `pi_light` (strip programs, UART link to the Pi);
- a **diode** so the Pi's 5 V can feed the ESP32 without being back-fed by its USB port;
- the **light strip connector**: data resistor, screw terminal and a 1000 µF capacitor.

It uses **four pins of the Pi's header**: 5 V (pin 2), GND (pin 6), TXD (pin 8) and RXD (pin 10).
Nothing else is connected, so it does not collide with what the Amp2 uses (pins 3, 5, 7, 12, 35, 38, 40).

:::note[Generated, not drawn by hand]
Every coordinate comes from
[`hardware/perfboard_pi3/perfboard.py`](https://github.com/bailleul-dev/robine-wake/tree/main/hardware/perfboard_pi3).
The script refuses a layout where two parts share a hole, so figures and tables cannot disagree.
:::

:::caution[Check these two things before you buy or solder]
1. **The Amp2 must expose the 40 pins on its top side** (a pass-through or stacking header long enough
   to take a second board). The HAT plugs on those pins; if your Amp2 has no pins on top, this HAT cannot sit
   above it. Measure the free pin length: the HAT's socket needs about 6 mm.
2. **The ESP32 devkit must have its two rows 9 holes (22.86 mm) apart**, 15 pins per row, the common CP2102
   "30-pin" board. A 38-pin DevKitC is wider and does not fit. The pin names below are the usual ones for this
   board: compare them with the silkscreen of yours before soldering the sockets.
:::

## What you need

- **Double-sided perfboard**, plated holes, 2.54 mm pitch, cut to **25 × 20 holes** (about 65 × 51 mm).
- The parts below, a soldering iron, a multimeter, a little Kapton tape.
- **Insulated 30 AWG wire**, about {{TOTAL}} m in total: red, black, white, gray.
- A 5 V supply for the strip (≥ 3 A for 60 LED), separate from the Pi.

{{PARTS}}

## Read this before you solder

:::danger[Rules]
- **Columns count from the left edge, rows from the top edge, seen from the component side.** A hole is written *(column, row)*; hole (1, 1) is the top-left corner. The header is on the **bottom edge**, pin 1 at (3, 20) on the lower of its two rows; the Pi's outer row (even pins) is the upper one.
- The **Pi socket hangs under the board**: its body is on the solder side, its pin tips come through on the component side. The ESP32 sockets, D1, R3, J4 and C1 are on the component side. **All wires run on the solder side**, except the four short links of step 1.
- When you flip the board, flip it **left to right** like a book page: column 1 ends up on the right. The solder-side figures are drawn that way.
- **The strip's current must not flow through the board.** The strip supply and the strip wires go into the **same clamps** of J4.
- Never put 5 V on a 3.3 V pin: only the four header pins listed here are wired.
:::

Only the strip along the header (rows 18 to 20) sits above the Amp2. Everything above the dashed line of the figures hangs
beyond the edge of the Pi, about 4 cm out: **hold the board** with a spacer, a rubber band or a drop of hot glue on the
Amp2's side if it is going to be handled. The ESP32's USB port ends up on the **left edge**, the side of the Pi's SD card, where nothing blocks the
cable. The strip leaves through the **top edge**. The antenna of the devkit points to the right, in the air: keep
the red zone free of parts and wires.

## Placement

<Perfboard svg={placement} />

### Solder the sockets

1. **Pi socket.** Plug the 2×20 female header on a spare 2×20 male header (or on the Amp2's pins), slide the board on from above so the pin tips come through, check that the socket sits flat against the **underside**, tack two corner pins, then solder all 40 pins on the component side.
2. **ESP32 sockets.** Push the two 1×15 female headers on the devkit's pins, drop them in their holes (rows 6 and 15, columns 4 to 18, pin 1 on the right), tack one pin at each end, then solder from the solder side.
3. Solder D1 (band toward the cathode), R3, J4 and C1 (long lead on +) at the holes of the table. R3 and C1 stand above the devkit, near J4.
4. **Cover the solder side of the strip along the header** with Kapton tape once everything is soldered and tested: it sits a few millimetres from the top of the Amp2.

## Step 1 — link the header pins to their feed holes

The socket body covers the pads of its own two rows on the solder side, so each pin you use is
**linked on the component side to a free hole just above it**, in row 18 (a *feed hole*, outside the body). The four pins used (2, 6, 8, 10)
are all on the outer row, so the wires of the next steps start from those holes and go **straight up**, toward the devkit:
nothing may run under the socket body.

<Perfboard svg={step1} />

{{LINKS}}

## Step 2 — power and UART

<Perfboard svg={step2} />

{{WIRES2}}

| ESP32 pin | Pi header pin | Role |
|---|---|---|
| VIN (through D1) | 2 | 5 V |
| GND | 6 | ground |
| D18 (GPIO 18, RX) | 8, TXD | Pi to ESP32 |
| TX2 (GPIO 17, TX) | 10, RXD | ESP32 to Pi |

**W4 and W5 cross once**, at (10, 16): lay the insulated wires one over the other, they only touch at that point. In step 3, **W10 also passes over W6** at (6, 5).

D1 sits between the Pi's 5 V and VIN: when you flash the ESP32 over its USB port, the USB 5 V cannot
feed the Pi's 5 V rail. The ESP32 gets about 4.7 V, which its regulator accepts.

## Step 3 — light strip

<Perfboard svg={step3} />

{{WIRES3}}

At J4, **each clamp takes two wires**:

| J4 clamp | Wire 1 | Wire 2 |
|---|---|---|
| +5V | strip supply + | strip +5 V |
| DATA | W7 (from R3) | strip Din |
| GND | strip supply − | strip GND |

The strip's current goes from its supply to the strip inside the clamps and never crosses the board.
The board only sees the data, the ground reference and the capacitor.

## Checks before stacking

Multimeter in continuity mode, **nothing plugged on the sockets, Pi off**.

| Test | Expected |
|---|---|
| header pin 2 ↔ header pin 6 (5 V ↔ GND) | **no beep**: this is the short that kills a Pi |
| header pin 6 ↔ ESP32 GND (both rows), J4 GND | beep |
| header pin 2 ↔ ESP32 VIN | the diode drops about 0.2 to 0.3 V in one direction only |
| header pin 8 ↔ ESP32 D18, header pin 10 ↔ ESP32 TX2 | beep |
| ESP32 D13 ↔ J4 DATA | about 330 Ω |
| J4 +5V ↔ J4 GND | no beep (the capacitor charges slowly) |
| header pins 1, 3 to 5, 7 and 9 to 40 ↔ anything of ours | no beep |

Then, in this order:

1. **Without the ESP32 devkit**, stack the HAT on the Amp2 and power the Pi. It must boot as before.
2. Power off, plug the **ESP32 devkit**, power on, flash it through its USB port (left edge) with `cargo run --release --bin pi_light`
   from `experimental/robine-wake-rs/firmware/esp32`. The build needs a `wifi.ini` file even though `pi_light` does not use Wi-Fi.
3. Connect the **strip and its supply**, then send a `light.program` from the web app.

## Software

Nothing changes on the Pi: the UART is the same (`ttyS0`, GPIO 14 and 15), and so is the configuration
(`guide_light: [driver: RobineWake.GuideLight.Driver.UART, device: "ttyS0"]`). The ESP32 firmware is the
one of the current setup: strip data on GPIO 13 (`D13`), link on UART2 with RX on GPIO 18 and TX on GPIO 17.
